## Reminders
{{ frame_doc }}
- A lighting / atmosphere / terrain / horizon change lives in `{{ env_file }}` (the world, the sun or
  moon lamp, the atmosphere volume, the ground and `height_at`, whatever continues the world).
- A composition/camera change is the plan's: `{{ entry_file }}` is harness data — move the SUBJECT
  (a zone's placement) rather than the camera.
- A zone change lives in its `src/zones/<zone>.py` (`def build(ctx)`, motion as keyframes on
  `ctx.frame(0.0)` / `ctx.frame(1.5)`); an asset change in `src/assets/<asset>.py`
  (`build_<asset>(ctx, variant=0)`).
- A dropped zone / asset (`zone_error`, `asset_error`): fix the named line; never wrap the whole
  builder in try/except to hide it.
- Everything rests on `ctx.height_at(x, y)` or on its support (move the root by the smallest
  `ctx.height_at(x, y) - z` over its foot band: the world-space vertices within max(0.02 m, 5 % of its height) of its lowest); nothing floats, sinks or runs through a
  neighbour (compare world bounding boxes).  No harness library ships: everything is raw `bpy` / `bmesh`
  (an import of `lib` is a lint ERROR); code zones share lives in `{{ env_file }}`.
- No world or cameras outside `{{ env_file }}`, no file IO, no `random.*`, no Python drivers.

## The recurring findings on this track — what each measures, and where the requirement is stated
Do not re-explain the problem back — change what the finding measures.  If a measured finding repeats,
change what it measures until its number passes; a repeated judge note needs a visible change in the
named view.  The skills named below are in your session's skill list; the contract is above.

| what the judge / gate wrote | the requirement (and how it is measured) | stated in |
|---|---|---|
| flat / one-colour ground, hard raised-bed edges | the surface needs visible variation that matches the brief's material, and its seams dressed (judge `flat_ground`) | the env brief § 1; skill `c3d-bpy-scene-materials` |
| a hard world edge against the sky, missing horizon | from no camera may the ground end against the sky (`world_edge_visible`; gates `world_edge` / `fog_short`); what continues the world is the brief's — street, sea, cliff, forest, wall, fog | contract § What else the gates measure; skill `c3d-scene-atmosphere` |
| the backdrop looks like flat / beige / untextured cut-outs | a distant backdrop needs volume and lighting consistent with the sky; aerial perspective is the fog's job, never a pre-tint (`placeholder_material`) | skill `c3d-scene-atmosphere` |
| a pond / terrace is a hard-edged plane sitting on the ground | water and terraces meet the ground without a visible seam: carving into `height_at` (`{{ env_file }}`) or dressing the seam (the zone) are options; water is named `…Water` | skill `c3d-scene-water`; skill `c3d-bpy-scene-placement` |
| `floating` / `sunken` / `interpenetration` / `unsupported` (the placement gate's metres) | move the root by the printed metres: its lowest evaluated vertices onto `ctx.height_at` or its support, boxes apart; a rock may sit a third in, a crate may not | contract § Placement; skill `c3d-bpy-scene-placement` |
| "camera is N m below ground level" / `camera_in_geometry` | the eye must be above the ground under it and ≥ 0.5 m from any surface: move the subject or the zone's placement; the landform is the brief's | contract § What else the gates measure |
| identical cones / stacked blocks / primitive repetition | identical copies and primitive stacks are what the judge reads (`monotonous_vegetation`); a tree branches into limbs carrying leaf or needle cards, a rock is irregular (`asset_quality_detail`); vary where the brief has natural variety, regular things stay regular | the zone brief § 2 |
| sparse, token, "a diorama on a plane", empty midground | the brief's stated counts are requirements (the census compares them); empty regions the brief would fill get what it implies (`undressed_scene`, `empty_midground`) | the zone brief § 1; skill `c3d-scene-composition` |
| flat / dark / monochrome light, black shade, `dark_frame` | measured per camera (`mean_lum` / `dark_frac` / `blown_frac`); the brief's time of day and light sources decide; the lighting skill lists starting values that have passed | skill `c3d-bpy-scene-lighting`; skill `c3d-scene-night` |
